{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cght6Vy6mXB3PoMYKnjYR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For K₃Cr(CN)₆ (octahedral Cr³⁺, d³), Dq ≈ 2660 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 529 cm⁻¹ and C ≈ 3000 cm⁻¹. Dq and B come from exact formulas; C relies on a common approximation for the ²E band.\n\n**1. Dq from the ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₂g band**\n\nFor d³ in an octahedral field, the energy of the ⁴T₂g band equals the ligand-field splitting:\n\nE(⁴T₂g) = 10Dq = 26 600 cm⁻¹, so **Dq = 2660 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**2. B from the ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₁g(F) band**\n\nThe ⁴T₁g(F) energy comes from the exact solution of the 2×2 matrix that mixes the two ⁴T₁g states:\n\nE(⁴T₁g) = 15Dq + 7.5B − ½·√(225B² − 180Dq·B + 100Dq²)\n\nSubstituting E = 32 500 cm⁻¹ and Dq = 2660 cm⁻¹:\n\n- 32 500 = 39 900 + 7.5B − ½√(225B² − 478 800B + 7.0756×10⁸)\n- Rearranging gives √(…) = 14 800 + 15B.\n- Squaring both sides: 7.0756×10⁸ − 478 800B = 2.1904×10⁸ + 444 000B\n- So 922 800B = 4.8852×10⁸, giving **B ≈ 529 cm⁻¹**.\n\nAs a check, β = 529/918 ≈ 0.58 (918 cm⁻¹ is B for the free Cr³⁺ ion). That is a strong nephelauxetic effect, which fits cyanide as a ligand.\n\n**3. C from the spin-forbidden ⁴A₂g → ²Eg band**\n\nI used the standard strong-field expression for the ²Eg energy. It takes the t₂g³ value (9B + 3C) and adds the second-order correction from mixing with the other ²E states:\n\nE(²E) ≈ 9B + 3C − 90B²/(10Dq)\n\n- 12 820 = 4765 + 3C − 90(529.4)²/26 600 = 4765 + 3C − 948\n- So 3C ≈ 9004, giving **C ≈ 3000 cm⁻¹**.\n\nThis gives C/B ≈ 5.7.\n\nBecause this formula is approximate, C is good to roughly ±100–200 cm⁻¹. I did not check it by diagonalising the full ²E matrix, which would give a somewhat different value. The values of Dq and B do not depend on this approximation.\n\n{\"Dq\": 2660, \"B\": 529, \"C\": 3000, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq from 4T2, B from exact 4T1(F) formula, C from approximate strong-field 2E expression.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_